{%- comment -%}
  Product card — the option control the design puts on the card itself.

  Picking a value moves the caption, the price and what goes in the bag — and
  deliberately *not* the photograph, which is the design's own rule: "Colour is
  chosen on the card itself: the name, the price and what goes in the bag all
  follow it. The photograph does not — we shoot one metal."

  Every control is a **real link** to the piece at that value, so a pick works
  without scripting — it simply navigates — and each value's caption and price
  markup is rendered onto the link by Liquid, so a pick costs no request and no
  arithmetic in the browser. (The design uses a `<button>`; a button without
  script does nothing, and the theme's rule is that nothing is load-bearing
  without scripting.)

  **There is no option picker here, and that is a structural finding rather
  than a simplification.** A theme block cannot read its parent's settings and
  cannot read a sibling's, so a merchant-set "which option" would be visible to
  this block alone — the price and the button resolve their variant
  independently, and would go on answering for the option they derive. The row
  would mark one value selected while the price beside it quoted another, with
  nothing on screen to say why. So all three derive the option identically
  through `snippets/card-option.liquid`. Same rule the quick view's axes are
  written to: "Which control an axis gets is derived, not tabled."

  What *is* a setting here is presentation, which nothing else depends on: how
  the values are drawn, and whether the chosen one is named above them.

  **Swatches paint from the option value's own Color metafield and from nowhere
  else.** `snippets/metal-swatch.liquid` is the single place the theme decides
  what a metal looks like, shared with the quick view and the product page. A
  value with no swatch configured paints nothing and keeps its hairline and its
  accessible name, so it stays pickable and announced — that is the connection
  missing in admin, not a theme fault. Never hardcode a table of metals here.

  The order is `snippets/metal-order.liquid`'s: the design's own metal table,
  not the order the option happens to carry, with anything it does not
  recognise appended rather than dropped. That is what makes it safe on an
  option that is not a metal at all — every value is unmatched, so the merchant's
  own order survives intact.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign product = closest.product

  capture card_option_raw
    render 'card-option', product: product
  endcapture
  assign card_option = card_option_raw | strip | split: '||'
  assign opt_index = card_option[0] | plus: 0
  assign selected_value = ''
  if card_option.size > 2
    assign selected_value = card_option[1]
  endif

  assign card_option_drop = blank
  if opt_index >= 0
    for option in product.options_with_values
      if forloop.index0 == opt_index
        assign card_option_drop = option
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  capture ordered_values_raw
    render 'metal-order', option: card_option_drop
  endcapture
  assign ordered_values = ordered_values_raw | strip | split: '||'

  assign is_pills = false
  if block.settings.control == 'pills'
    assign is_pills = true
  endif

  comment
    **The caption is the design's own — "White Gold · 1.0 ct" — and the carat
    half belongs here rather than to a `product-card-meta` block.** It reads
    like a second fact and is not one: the design states the caption as
    `metalName(sel) + ' · ' + carat`, one line describing the piece *as
    configured*, and only this block knows what was picked. Split onto a line of
    its own the carat stops following the caption it qualifies, and Most Loved
    would say less than the same card says on a collection page.

    `product-card-meta` is for what the piece is regardless of the pick, which
    is why it is additive rather than where this lives.

    No karat prefix. It read "22K White Gold · 1.0 ct" once, which said the
    karat twice — the "Available in" block already lists every karat the piece
    comes in, and the card cannot choose one anyway.
  endcomment
  assign carat_label = product.metafields.custom.total_carat_weight.value | append: ''
  if carat_label != blank
    unless carat_label contains 'ct'
      assign carat_label = carat_label | append: ' ct'
    endunless
  endif

  comment
    **Joined with `append`, not inside a `capture`.** Written as a capture, the
    separator sits between two whitespace-stripping tag delimiters, and those
    dashes eat the spaces either side of it: the caption came out
    "Yellow Gold·1.5 ct", while the identical string built for the swatch links
    read correctly because those tags happened not to strip. Either half can be
    missing and "· 1.5 ct" with nothing before it is not a caption, so the join
    is conditional. Same idiom `snippets/product-card.liquid` uses.
  endcomment
  assign selected_caption = selected_value
  if carat_label != blank
    if selected_caption != blank
      assign selected_caption = selected_caption | append: ' · ' | append: carat_label
    else
      assign selected_caption = carat_label
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The chosen value's name. Without a control it is a fixed string and says so
  with `data-card-meta-fixed`, which is what stops `theme.js` rewriting an
  element nothing can change — the same marker the metafield-fed caption
  carries in `snippets/product-card.liquid`.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if block.settings.show_name -%}
  <span
    class="card__meta"
    data-card-meta
    {% if card_option_drop == blank %}data-card-meta-fixed{% endif %}
    {% if card_option_drop == blank %}{{ block.shopify_attributes }}{% endif %}
  >{{ selected_caption }}</span>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if card_option_drop != blank -%}
  <div
    class="{% if is_pills %}card__pills{% else %}card__swatches{% endif %}"
    {% unless is_pills %}data-card-swatches{% endunless %}
    {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
  >
    {%- for value in ordered_values -%}
      {%- liquid
        comment
          The card offers no karat or size control, so a pick has to choose a
          variant on the customer's behalf: the dearest *available* one carrying
          this value. A sold-out variant cannot be bought, so preferring it
          would post something that fails at checkout; where the whole value is
          sold out the dearest overall stands in and the button says Sold out.

          This is `card-option.liquid`'s rule applied per value rather than to
          the selected one, which is why it is written out here — the snippet
          answers for the card's current state, and the row has to answer for
          every state the card could be put into.
        endcomment
        assign chosen = blank
        assign dearest_any = blank
        for variant in product.variants
          if variant.options[opt_index] == value
            if dearest_any == blank or variant.price > dearest_any.price
              assign dearest_any = variant
            endif
            if variant.available
              if chosen == blank or variant.price > chosen.price
                assign chosen = variant
              endif
            endif
          endif
        endfor
        if chosen == blank
          assign chosen = dearest_any
        endif

        assign is_selected = false
        if value == selected_value
          assign is_selected = true
        endif

        comment
          `ordered_values` came back through `split`, so `value` is a plain
          string and no longer the product_option_value drop. The drop is what
          carries a merchant's native Shopify swatch, so it is looked up again
          here — without this the swatch snippet's first and most specific
          branch is silently unreachable.
        endcomment
        assign value_drop = blank
        for candidate in card_option_drop.values
          if candidate == value
            assign value_drop = candidate
            break
          endif
        endfor

        comment
          The caption this value would give, rendered onto the link so a pick
          needs no string in JavaScript. Same composition as the selected one,
          and built the same way for the same reason.
        endcomment
        assign value_caption = value
        if carat_label != blank
          assign value_caption = value_caption | append: ' · ' | append: carat_label
        endif
      -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The price markup a pick writes into `[data-card-price]`. It carries the
        figure and nothing else: `theme.js` replaces that element's whole
        `innerHTML`, and the tax note is a sibling block out of its reach — see
        `blocks/product-card-price.liquid`.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture value_price_html -%}
        {%- if chosen.compare_at_price > chosen.price -%}
          <s>{{ chosen.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
        {%- endif -%}
        {{- chosen.price | money -}}
      {%- endcapture -%}

      <a
        class="{% if is_pills %}card__pill{% else %}card__swatch{% endif %}{% if is_selected %} is-selected{% endif %}"
        href="{{ product.url }}?variant={{ chosen.id }}"
        data-card-metal
        data-metal-value="{{ value | escape }}"
        data-metal-id="{{ chosen.id }}"
        data-metal-available="{{ chosen.available }}"
        data-metal-meta="{{ value_caption | escape }}"
        data-metal-price="{{ value_price_html | escape }}"
        {% unless is_pills %}data-tip="{{ value | escape }}"{% endunless %}
        aria-label="{{ value | escape }}"
        aria-current="{% if is_selected %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
        {% if is_pills == false and forloop.index0 >= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
      >
        {%- comment -%}
          `selected` is deliberately not passed to the dot. The wrapping link
          carries the state and `.card__swatch.is-selected .swatch-dot` draws
          the ring from it, so there is one source of truth. Marking the dot as
          well left two rings after a pick: the script moves the class on the
          link and never on the dot.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if is_pills -%}
          {{- value -}}
        {%- else -%}
          {% render 'metal-swatch', option_value: value_drop, size: 80 %}
        {%- endif -%}
      </a>
    {%- endfor -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The design's "+N". Before a width is known it shows three —
      `else if (!avail) n = Math.min(keys.length, 3)` — so that is what Liquid
      renders, and the script re-fits once it can measure. Pills wrap instead of
      clipping, so they need none of it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- unless is_pills -%}
      {%- assign value_total = ordered_values.size -%}
      <span
        class="card__swatch-more"
        data-card-swatch-more
        {% if value_total <= 3 %}hidden{% endif %}
      >{% if value_total > 3 %}+{{ value_total | minus: 3 }}{% endif %}</span>
    {%- endunless -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.name",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "control",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "swatches", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.swatches" },
        { "value": "pills", "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.control.pills" }
      ],
      "default": "swatches"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_name",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.show_name.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.show_name.info",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "t:blocks.product_card_variants.name",
      "settings": {
        "control": "swatches",
        "show_name": true
      }
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
